@<Module@/Go Project@=
{"description": "Structures of a whole Go module"}
@

A requirement is one line of the require block: a module path and a version.
No require block is emitted when the list is empty or contains only whitespace.
A project with requirements also declares go.sum in the file set go module,
because the checks never rewrite go.mod or go.sum.
@<Go Project@/go.mod@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my %meta = @<.@/project meta information@>;
die "go version '$meta{go}' is not valid\n"
    if $meta{go} !~ /^1\.\d+(\.\d+)?$/;
my $requirements = <<'LITAR_END_REQUIREMENTS';
@<.@/requirements@>
LITAR_END_REQUIREMENTS
$requirements =~ s/\A\s+//;
$requirements =~ s/\s+\z//;
print "module @<.@/module path@>\n\ngo $meta{go}\n";
print "\nrequire (\n$requirements\n)\n" if $requirements ne '';
@|Basic@/evaluate@

The import path of a package is the module path joined with its directory.
Reference it with the package label, inside quotes in import paths.
A command needs no directory here, because no package imports a command.
@<Go Project@/import path@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my $dir = <<'LITAR_END_DIRECTORY';
@<.@/package directory@>
LITAR_END_DIRECTORY
$dir =~ s/\A\s+//;
$dir =~ s/\s+\z//;
die "package directory '$dir' is not valid\n"
    if $dir =~ m{^/|/$|//|\s}
    || grep { $_ eq '.' || $_ eq '..' } split m{/}, $dir;
my $module = '@<.@/module path@>';
print $dir eq '' ? $module : "$module/$dir";
@|Basic@/evaluate@

@<Go Project@/module path@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my %meta = @<.@/project meta information@>;
die "module path '$meta{module}' is not valid\n"
    if $meta{module} !~ m{^[^\s/]\S*$};
print $meta{module};
@|Basic@/evaluate@

A Go project is one Go module: a go.mod file at the root of the module,
and packages in directories below it.
One node holds one project, so the blocks of this module carry no label,
and every file of the node sees the same module.
A package is a label, as in Go Structure,
and each file of a package carries that label with a label of its own.
A command is a package whose rightmost label is command;
by convention its files live in cmd, in a directory named after the command.
A test file is one more file of its package, named after the file it tests with _test,
and it adds testing to its own import paths.
Declare go.mod and every Go file in the file set go module,
so the project checks below see the whole project.

The module entry is the module path, and the go entry is the minimum Go version.
example.com/project stands in until a project sets its own path.
A later entry replaces an earlier entry with the same key,
so a project sets its entries in project properties.
@<Go Project@/project meta information@=
(
    module => 'example.com/project',
    go => '1.24',
    @<.@/project properties@>
)
@

The package directory is the path of a package below the module root, without a leading slash.
A package sets it under the package label.
The package at the module root keeps the empty directory.
@<Go Project@/package directory@=@

@<Go Project@/Filters@/build packages@=#!/bin/sh
@<.@/go command settings@>
go build ./...
echo 'go build ./...: ok'
@

@<Go Project@/Filters@/vet packages@=#!/bin/sh
@<.@/go command settings@>
go vet ./...
echo 'go vet ./...: ok'
@

@<Go Project@/Filters@/test packages@=#!/bin/sh
@<.@/go command settings@>
go test ./...
@

Each check runs the go command in a view of the file set go module,
so the module root is the working directory.
GOTOOLCHAIN=local keeps the go command from downloading another toolchain,
and GOWORK=off ignores a workspace file outside the view.
The build cache and the module cache stay at their host locations.
A failed check fails the evaluation, and the go command reports on stderr.
@<Go Project@/Filters@/go command settings@=
set -e
export GOTOOLCHAIN=local
export GOWORK=off
@

@<Module@/Go Project@/Filters@=
{"description": "Run the go command on the files of the project."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/project meta information@=
{"description": "Module path, Go version, and other properties, as a Perl hash."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/project properties@=
{"description": "Extra Perl hash entries for the project meta information."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/module path@=
{"description": "Module path from the project meta information."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/requirements@=
{"description": "Lines of the require block of go.mod."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/go.mod@=
{"description": "Contents of go.mod for the project."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/package directory@=
{"description": "Directory of a package below the module root."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/import path@=
{"description": "Import path of a package, without quotes."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/check report@=
{"description": "Output of go build, go vet, and go test on the file set go module."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/Filters@/go command settings@=
{"description": "Shell lines that pin the environment of the go command."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/Filters@/build packages@=
{"description": "Build every package of the project."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/Filters@/vet packages@=
{"description": "Vet every package of the project."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Project@/Filters@/test packages@=
{"description": "Test every package of the project."}
@

The check report runs every check in turn and stops at the first failure.
The output of a check ends with a line break; it is removed,
so the line break after each reference separates the checks.
@<Go Project@/check report@=
@<@|Go Project@/Filters@/build packages@+go module@|Text Processing@/remove ending newlines@>
@<@|Go Project@/Filters@/vet packages@+go module@|Text Processing@/remove ending newlines@>
@<@|Go Project@/Filters@/test packages@+go module@|Text Processing@/remove ending newlines@>
@
